actors: Browser | Server

step: The browser asks for a token, using its session
msg: Browser -> Server: POST /token + session cookie
text: The JWT in this lab sits on top of lab 00's session. You prove who you
  are with the cookie, and the server turns that into a token.

step: The server signs three chunks and sends them back
msg: Server -> Browser: header.payload.signature
note: Server: signs with JWT_SECRET
text: The header and payload are plain JSON, base64url encoded. The signature
  is computed over both with a key only the server holds. That key never
  leaves the server.

step: The browser reads the claims without asking anyone
note: Browser: decodes locally
text: Anyone can do this, with no secret at all. The decoder panel in the app
  does exactly this. Reading a token tells you what it claims, and nothing
  about whether to believe it.

step: Someone edits the payload, or the signature
note: Browser: one character changed
text: A hand-edited token still decodes fine. The browser can't tell, and
  doesn't need to, because it was never the one deciding.

step: The server is asked to verify the token
msg: Browser -> Server: POST /verify { token }
text: Only the server can answer this, because only the server has the secret.

step: The server rejects it for a specific reason
msg: Server -> Browser: valid: false, "invalid signature"
note: Server: recomputes the signature
text: It recomputes the signature and compares. A mismatch is "invalid
  signature". Expiry and missing claims have their own reasons, and the
  signature has to be checked first because a forged token can claim any
  `exp` it likes. The starter skips that check, which is the gap in this
  lab.
